WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.020
What's up? Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 47. It's May 23rd, 2026.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:12.060
Some awesome AI developments today, and we're going to make all of it make sense. Let's get into it.

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:17.420
So imagine you are working on a school coding project, and you want help from an AI.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:22.920
Instead of typing out every detail about your screen, what if the AI could just look at your screen

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:28.560
with one click? That is exactly what OpenAI just added to its coding tool called Codex.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:34.160
The new feature is called AppShots, and it lets you send a live view of whatever is on your max

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:39.740
screen straight to the AI. Think of it like handing your AI helper a photograph of your workspace

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:45.740
instead of writing a long letter describing every file and error message. Another way to picture

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:50.760
it is like having a tutor who can peek over your shoulder while you work, rather than forcing you

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:56.800
to explain everything from memory. This matters because most coding help right now requires copy and

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:02.680
paste steps that are easy to mess up or forget. Students learning to code or people building small

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:08.880
hobby apps can now get advice that matches their exact project without extra typing. The result feels

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:14.240
more like chatting with someone who can see the same thing you see. If you have a Mac and access to

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:20.440
Codex through chat GPT, open a coding window and try the AppShot shortcut to watch how the answers

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:26.020
change with real screen context. The feature is rolling out now for Mac users.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:31.180
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, the deep dive where we look under the hood.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:37.940
Today, we are unpacking how large language models actually turn simple math into writing essays or code.

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:43.260
You know how your phone suggests the next word while you text. Now picture that same

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:48.160
autocomplete idea study billions of sentences instead of just your recent chats.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:54.000
The model does not think like a person, it turns every word into a number called a token

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.300
and uses math to guess which number comes next based on patterns it saw during training.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:04.140
Think of each word as a tiny arrow in space that points toward its meaning,

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:10.580
like placing puzzle pieces on a giant map, then a part called attention looks at which arrows relate

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:15.260
to each other, similar to how you notice which ingredients matter most in a recipe.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:21.660
Finally, a section called the LM head turns all that math back into real words you can read.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:27.920
There is no sudden moment when it becomes aware. It stays an enormous probability machine

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:33.560
that keeps picking the best next token. And that is basically how large language models work.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:39.080
Not so scary, right? Let's move to some cool stuff you can actually try. First up,

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:45.460
researchers at Anthropic found that their clawed models spotted over 10,000 serious security holes

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:51.440
in important software. The issue is, these problems are showing up faster than teams can fix them.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:57.600
Think of it like a smoke detector that rings before the fire is fully out, leaving a risky gap.

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:03.540
This matters because the same kind of AI that helps with homework could also be used to find weak

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:08.820
spots in apps you use every day. While you cannot try the preview model yourself,

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:14.160
you can stay safer by keeping your own apps and devices updated whenever new patches appear.

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:21.500
Next, Google AI overviews sometimes ignore the actual words you typed and give a generic answer instead.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:28.200
One test showed that searching the single word disregard made the system respond like a casual

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:33.920
chatbot rather than a search summary. Picture a librarian who starts telling stories instead of

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:39.780
finding the book you asked for. This shows that even big AI tools still have rough edges when

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:45.940
handling real requests. You can test it yourself right now by going to google.com and trying a simple

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:52.660
one word search like disregard to see what the AI overview shows. Time for some quick bits.

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:59.020
A new report shows that Elon Musk's GROC chatbot barely appears in records of how the US government

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:05.640
used AI tools last year. It is a reminder that building a chatbot is one thing, but getting people

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:11.440
to actually use it every day is much harder. Researchers are also pointing out that tools like chat

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:18.300
GPT continue to repeat or create false political claims in some cases. This is a useful heads-up for

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:23.880
anyone using AI to research current events or school topics since the answers can sound confident

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:29.440
even when they are wrong. And that's a wrap. If any of today's stories made you go, huh? That's

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:35.760
cool. Go play with it. Curiosity is how every expert started. See you tomorrow. And if you'd rather

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:40.660
watch then listen, find us on YouTube at At Narrow Network, links in the show notes.

